// 00 · hello — meet the medium
//
// This is the smallest Mentl program that does something you can watch: run
// it and three greetings print. That is the whole program. The rest of this
// comment matters more than usual, because in Mentl a comment is not thrown
// away — the lexer keeps it as a Reason edge on the graph, so the medium can
// read your intent back to you.
//
// Mentl has one kernel: one graph, two operations (draw an edge, project a
// read). At any point in your code the medium reads that graph eight ways —
// its type, the handlers over it, the verb that shapes it, its effect row,
// its ownership, its refinements, the gradient's next move, and the Reason it
// holds. The eight files after this one take those one at a time. This one
// just says hello.
//
// Run it:   mentl run  lib/tutorial/00-hello.mn
// Edit it:  Mentl Space — the page in ide/, live at https://space.ampactor.dev/ (this lesson is in its program list)
// Read it:  mentl lib/tutorial/00-hello.mn:29:77   ← ask at the pipe into print_string; the medium answers
import io

// greet builds a line and flows it into print_string with `|>`, the
// sequential verb: the value on the left becomes the argument on the right.
// `++` joins byte-sequences. Joining allocates, so greet's effect row carries
// Memory and Alloc — Mentl infers that from the body; you never wrote it down.
// (Ask for `with !Alloc` here and the medium refuses to build the program,
// naming this declaration: it proves the join allocates, and a claim it
// proves false produces no program at all. Declaring and denying effects is
// the subject of 04-row.)
fn greet(name) = "Hello, " ++ name ++ ". The medium is reading you back.\n" |> print_string

fn main() = {
  greet("kernel")
  greet("oracle")
  greet("octopus")
}
